00:13:47,242 --> 00:13:50,824
Then keep an eye on that bar because
you'll see one, another one in a

223
00:13:50,825 --> 00:13:53,364
few minutes that's gonna look really
different when we get into some

224
00:13:53,364 --> 00:13:54,865
of the other experiments I ran.

225
00:13:55,865 --> 00:13:59,804
Okay, so remember the ties that
I briefly mentioned earlier?

226
00:14:00,542 --> 00:14:02,085
So let's come back to that now.

227
00:14:02,481 --> 00:14:03,583
This is kind of crazy.

228
00:14:04,032 --> 00:14:06,780
sometimes the judge give,
gives both responses the

229
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:08,203
same score, and that's a tie.

230
00:14:08,216 --> 00:14:10,389
And as you know, we can't
order the tie, right?

231
00:14:11,006 --> 00:14:12,907
So the tie doesn't
count as right or wrong.

232
00:14:12,931 --> 00:14:14,383
There's no order to check.

233
00:14:14,695 --> 00:14:18,493
Now, this creates another problem that
we need to take into account as we're

234
00:14:18,495 --> 00:14:24,384
comparing the two different methodologies
for, for sending this to the judge, right?

235
00:14:24,684 --> 00:14:31,496
Because say that packing made the judge
tie more often on the hard examples,

236
00:14:31,557 --> 00:14:34,871
what would happen in my analysis is those
examples would just drop out of the count.

237
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:38,217
So accuracy would still look
fine, but only because we kind of

238
00:14:38,230 --> 00:14:39,899
dropped out the hard ones, right?

239
00:14:40,331 --> 00:14:41,252
So I checked that.

240
00:14:41,295 --> 00:14:41,552
Right.

241
00:14:41,626 --> 00:14:47,149
The judge tied about 48% of the time
with one metric per call and about 48%

242
00:14:47,150 --> 00:14:49,373
of the time with five metrics per call.

243
00:14:49,374 --> 00:14:50,923
So these are basically the same.

244
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:56,686
Packing didn't make the judge
any more or less indecisive, and

245
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:58,472
this is a really high tie rate.

246
00:14:58,472 --> 00:15:00,524
I was actually super
surprised to see this.

247
00:15:00,865 --> 00:15:05,181
It could be because I was using a
smaller cost-effective model, but it

248
00:15:05,181 --> 00:15:08,611
also could be because I'm using a zero
to four-point scale, so I'm not giving

249
00:15:08,612 --> 00:15:12,802
the judge much leeway in terms of,
like, where to land, between these two.

250
00:15:13,650 --> 00:15:17,211
So when you think of a zero to
four-point scale, plenty of responses

251
00:15:17,212 --> 00:15:21,361
can land on that same score, and
even people might have a hard time

252
00:15:21,363 --> 00:15:22,905
telling, you know, the two apart.

253
00:15:23,459 --> 00:15:27,950
So that's why I always put the tie
rate down when I, you know, present

254
00:15:27,954 --> 00:15:30,904
these results, and I recommend you do
the same because accuracy will tell

255
00:15:30,904 --> 00:15:35,174
you how often the judge is right when
it does pick, but the tie rate tells

256
00:15:35,176 --> 00:15:37,198
you how often it couldn't pick at all.

257
00:15:38,198 --> 00:15:39,983
Okay, so five metrics was awesome.

258
00:15:41,426 --> 00:15:43,921
but I also wanted to know
about 10, 20, and 40.

259
00:15:44,019 --> 00:15:48,042
Now, HelpSteer2, the data set I
introduced at the beginning, it only

260
00:15:48,055 --> 00:15:52,469
has human scores for five things,
and I really didn't have the time

261
00:15:52,470 --> 00:15:57,089
to invent 30 more and go out and get
human labels on them, especially with

262
00:15:57,089 --> 00:15:58,663
the rigor that they did at NVIDIA.

263
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:02,701
So I was like, "What else could
I do to get more ground truth?"

264
00:16:03,250 --> 00:16:09,565
So what I did was I broke the answers on
purpose, so this is synthetic corruption.

265
00:16:10,565 --> 00:16:14,174
So basically what I did was I took a clean
response from the data set, and then I

266
00:16:14,175 --> 00:16:17,061
made a small and specific change to it.

267
00:16:17,544 --> 00:16:21,314
and because I made the change, I know
which version is worse, and I know

268
00:16:21,348 --> 00:16:23,379
which metric should notice that change.

269
00:16:24,074 --> 00:16:27,562
So these are some examples of some real
edits to the data set, so you can kind of

270
00:16:27,563 --> 00:16:29,389
get a better idea of what I mean by that.

271
00:16:29,886 --> 00:16:34,487
some of them are kind of fun, like the
filler one takes a haiku about a banana

272
00:16:35,155 --> 00:16:40,029
and starts it with, "It should be borne in
mind that here is a haiku about a banana,"

273
00:16:40,691 --> 00:16:43,420
and so that should hurt conciseness.

274
00:16:43,894 --> 00:16:47,331
and the grammar one, for example,
turns, "I do not accept it,"

275
00:16:47,344 --> 00:16:49,844
to, "I does not accept it."

276
00:16:51,098 --> 00:16:55,897
the repeated sentence one turns a list of
hashtags into, "Data mining, data mining."

277
00:16:56,002 --> 00:16:59,968
the shuffle one takes a bad
math answer and puts the

278
00:16:59,987 --> 00:17:01,790
explanation before the equation.

279
00:17:03,229 --> 00:17:07,511
And the truncate one cuts a poem
about a dog off right in the middle.

280
00:17:07,528 --> 00:17:11,212
So the deleted answer one is
another one that I threw up here.

281
00:17:11,618 --> 00:17:14,977
For this one, it removes the
sentence that tells you how to fix

282
00:17:14,982 --> 00:17:16,725
the error you actually asked about.

283
00:17:17,601 --> 00:17:22,746
And each edit targets one metric, as I
mentioned, so repetition should make an

284
00:17:22,750 --> 00:17:27,678
answer more verbose, missing punctuation
should hurt the punctuation score, a

285
00:17:27,679 --> 00:17:32,110
cutoff ending should hurt completeness,
and there's one more on the end.

286
00:17:32,133 --> 00:17:39,969
The dashed one, it swaps a number for a
wrong one, like changing 2,000 to 4,000.

287
00:17:39,980 --> 00:17:44,561
So that one tests correctness,
and I kept that as another check.

288
00:17:45,673 --> 00:17:48,178
so instead of asking the judge,
"How good is this answer?"

289
00:17:48,191 --> 00:17:50,494
I'm asking, "Did you
notice the thing I broke?"

290
00:17:50,544 --> 00:17:51,210
essentially.

291
00:17:52,041 --> 00:17:53,955
And that has a clear right answer.

292
00:17:53,977 --> 00:17:56,686
I don't need to get any human
labels, and that's how I used

293
00:17:56,687 --> 00:17:59,183
it to increase my rubric size

294
00:18:00,183 --> 00:18:02,267
And now here's how the
broken answer becomes a test.

295
00:18:02,350 --> 00:18:05,913
Every corruption is declared
in one place in the code.

296
00:18:06,435 --> 00:18:08,874
On the left is the one
that repeats the sentences.

297
00:18:08,889 --> 00:18:12,767
You can take a look at that example,
and it says three things: which

298
00:18:12,812 --> 00:18:18,321
metric it targets, verbosity in this
case, and which direction the score

299
00:18:18,321 --> 00:18:23,472
should move, up, and how hard to push,
three repeated sentences by default.

300
00:18:23,662 --> 00:18:24,566
And that's it.

301
00:18:24,576 --> 00:18:28,131
So the rest of the pipeline reads
from there, and nothing about the

302
00:18:28,131 --> 00:18:31,362
expected answer is decided, later.

303
00:18:32,302 --> 00:18:35,469
And on the right is how
each test pair gets built.

304
00:18:35,841 --> 00:18:40,029
So the clean version and the broken
version always come from the same

305
00:18:40,108 --> 00:18:44,377
original response, so the difference
between the two is only the edit I made.

306
00:18:44,740 --> 00:18:48,613
And then basically like a coin
flip from a seed decides which

307
00:18:48,614 --> 00:18:50,236
side gets the broken version.

308
00:18:51,261 --> 00:18:54,299
So plenty of judges have a slight
preference for whatever answer they

309
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:58,983
see first, and this way I'm able to,
randomize that and eliminate that

310
00:18:59,235 --> 00:19:01,342
so it can't help or hurt my score.

311
00:19:02,103 --> 00:19:05,577
And then every pair records
exactly how it was made, the

312
00:19:05,577 --> 00:19:09,004
operation, the target metric, the
expected direction, and the seed.

313
00:19:09,390 --> 00:19:12,565
So you should be able to rebuild
the whole data set from the repo if

314
00:19:12,570 --> 00:19:14,188
you want to add your own corruption.

315
00:19:14,606 --> 00:19:18,993
You can write one function and one
declaration and plug it right in

316
00:19:19,993 --> 00:19:21,729
Okay, so now the big test.

317
00:19:22,919 --> 00:19:26,540
In every call, there's one
metric I'm measuring, the one

318
00:19:26,542 --> 00:19:27,670
that the edit was aimed at.

319
00:19:28,465 --> 00:19:32,570
Every other metric is there to make the
call bigger, and at the top end, that's

320
00:19:32,583 --> 00:19:37,039
39 extra, extra metrics that I send
the judge and tell it it has to judge

321
00:19:37,039 --> 00:19:40,938
on that are really just along for the
ride in terms of how I'm going to score

322
00:19:40,938 --> 00:19:43,282
this, how I'm going to analyze this.

323
00:19:43,821 --> 00:19:46,008
so you can see this
sort of in the picture.

324
00:19:46,456 --> 00:19:52,686
each row is one step from one
up to 40, and the green block

325
00:19:52,717 --> 00:19:56,488
is the metric I'm measuring, and
the gray blocks are the extras.

326
00:19:56,509 --> 00:20:02,801
They come from the same list every
time, so the call at 10 contains

327
00:20:02,827 --> 00:20:04,677
the call at five plus five more.

328
00:20:04,678 --> 00:20:06,001
The list just keeps getting longer.

329
00:20:07,117 --> 00:20:09,898
Okay, so one rule holds at every step.

330
00:20:09,926 --> 00:20:13,557
The metric I'm measuring always stays
first, and that's that lone of…

331
00:20:13,579 --> 00:20:19,010
line of code at the left that I showed
you that's green, and then the extras.

332
00:20:20,543 --> 00:20:25,159
And remember the position idea from
earlier, that maybe things at the

333
00:20:25,160 --> 00:20:27,044
bottom of the list get less attention.

334
00:20:27,607 --> 00:20:30,755
Keeping the target at the top
takes that off the table, and if

335
00:20:30,755 --> 00:20:33,877
the score drops, it isn't because
the metric slid down a long list.

336
00:20:33,894 --> 00:20:34,689
It never moves.

337
00:20:35,375 --> 00:20:38,665
The only thing growing is the
pile of metrics behind it.

338
00:20:39,008 --> 00:20:45,012
So eight kinds of edits, each with
about 60 prompts, and no single

339
00:20:45,014 --> 00:20:46,677
kind of edit can dominate the result

340
00:20:47,677 --> 00:20:49,917
Okay, so what happens at 40?

341
00:20:50,880 --> 00:20:52,598
The judge does get worse.

342
00:20:53,076 --> 00:20:57,724
with 40 metrics in the call, it caught
the plan to change about six points

343
00:20:57,785 --> 00:20:59,817
less often than when it only had one.

344
00:21:00,707 --> 00:21:04,335
compare the bar here at the bottom
from the one that we saw earlier.

345
00:21:05,003 --> 00:21:06,077
This one…

346
00:21:06,427 --> 00:21:08,108
That one was crossing zero.

347
00:21:08,111 --> 00:21:09,398
This one doesn't even come close.

348
00:21:09,401 --> 00:21:11,719
The whole range is on the negative side.

349
00:21:12,392 --> 00:21:17,958
So, this came from more than 7,000 calls
that I measured this on, and the me-

350
00:21:18,187 --> 00:21:19,659
the metric I was measuring was first.

351
00:21:19,677 --> 00:21:21,619
The judge read it before
it read anything else.

352
00:21:23,023 --> 00:21:26,587
So stacking a long list of other
questions behind it made the

353
00:21:26,587 --> 00:21:28,407
judge worse at the first question.

354
00:21:30,089 --> 00:21:31,666
The total size matters.

355
00:21:31,734 --> 00:21:37,140
So in this run, a few calls
fail or come back garbled.

356
00:21:37,495 --> 00:21:40,156
I actually saw that more
often with the larger calls.

357
00:21:40,892 --> 00:21:45,279
so I handled those in four different
ways, from kind of forgiving to more

358
00:21:45,279 --> 00:21:51,584
strict, and regardless of that, this
drop showed up every way I counted it.

359
00:21:52,592 --> 00:21:54,833
So the cost at 40 metrics…

360
00:21:56,267 --> 00:21:56,595
Excuse me.

361
00:21:56,606 --> 00:21:57,224
The cost…

362
00:21:57,940 --> 00:22:02,604
So at 40 metrics, the cost of packing
shows up in the numbers very clearly.

363
00:22:02,616 --> 00:22:09,000
Six points is the kind of change
that would make me base a decision

364
00:22:09,019 --> 00:22:10,820
on how I would manage my judges.

365
00:22:11,361 --> 00:22:16,222
So I think this is very impactful Okay,
so what did you pick at the start?

366
00:22:16,243 --> 00:22:18,695
Was it 10, 20 or was it 40?

367
00:22:19,271 --> 00:22:25,315
I kept running these tests looking for,
you know, a cliff where this, accuracy

368
00:22:25,335 --> 00:22:29,906
would kind of fall off after a certain
point, but I didn't really find it.

369
00:22:30,041 --> 00:22:32,760
It was sort of a slow and gradual decline.

370
00:22:33,838 --> 00:22:37,262
latency looks fine, looks fine,
looks fine, and then falls over.

371
00:22:37,263 --> 00:22:39,746
Like, that's kind of the typical
pattern that we see when we

372
00:22:39,746 --> 00:22:41,091
run like a load test, right?

373
00:22:41,471 --> 00:22:42,643
That's what I was looking for here.

374
00:22:42,895 --> 00:22:44,673
but I didn't see it.

375
00:22:44,674 --> 00:22:46,094
I didn't see a sharp drop.

376
00:22:46,218 --> 00:22:47,782
that's not really what
happened in this case.

377
00:22:47,783 --> 00:22:52,466
Instead, it kind of slowly bent a
little worse at 10, a little worse at

378
00:22:52,468 --> 00:22:57,518
20, a little worse at 30 and 40, and
so every metric you add costs a little.

379
00:22:57,900 --> 00:23:02,696
In my setup, that works out to
roughly 1% lower odds of catching

380
00:23:02,697 --> 00:23:05,109
the change for every metric you add.

381
00:23:05,518 --> 00:23:10,933
So, I fitted it to a straight line
essentially So that bump that you see

382
00:23:10,934 --> 00:23:13,062
at five, I think that's just noise.

383
00:23:13,119 --> 00:23:14,340
I wouldn't read anything into it.

384
00:23:14,349 --> 00:23:17,966
And also note that this, is,
chart is very, very zoomed in.

385
00:23:18,264 --> 00:23:20,955
the bottom of it sits at, like, .7,

386
00:23:20,961 --> 00:23:22,549
I think, so it's not at zero.

387
00:23:22,842 --> 00:23:25,272
So I zoomed in here so you
could see the shape better.

388
00:23:26,446 --> 00:23:30,300
and, you know, whatever number you
guessed, I think is probably a fair guess.

389
00:23:30,306 --> 00:23:31,470
I was guessing, too.

390
00:23:31,889 --> 00:23:34,256
but it doesn't seem like there's,
like, a magic size where you're,

391
00:23:34,257 --> 00:23:35,985
like, safe to pack 'em in.

392
00:23:36,283 --> 00:23:39,976
You're constantly kind of losing
a little bit of accuracy the

393
00:23:39,978 --> 00:23:41,120
more that you add in there

394
00:23:42,120 --> 00:23:44,720
Okay, so that six-point drop
that I mentioned, that was the

395
00:23:44,735 --> 00:23:46,437
average across all kinds of edits.

396
00:23:46,438 --> 00:23:48,213
We kind of went through
those edits at the…

397
00:23:48,232 --> 00:23:51,243
when I was showing you how I did
the corrupted, synthetic corruption.

398
00:23:51,828 --> 00:23:54,774
But when I split it apart, it
was kind of interesting to see.

399
00:23:54,786 --> 00:23:58,175
So three kinds of those
edits were harder to spot.

400
00:23:58,289 --> 00:24:02,186
Repeated sentences took the biggest hit.

401
00:24:02,691 --> 00:24:05,579
With one metric in the call,
the judge caught the repetition

402
00:24:05,585 --> 00:24:06,978
about two-thirds of the time.

403
00:24:07,330 --> 00:24:10,442
With 40, it caught it less
than one-third of the time.

404
00:24:10,734 --> 00:24:12,664
That's kind of interesting,
and it sort of makes sense.

405
00:24:13,101 --> 00:24:17,004
broken grammar and missing
punctuation also dropped a lot.

406
00:24:18,170 --> 00:24:22,534
And these are kind of like small
local edits, like one repeated line,

407
00:24:22,534 --> 00:24:24,887
one wrong verb, a few missing commas.

408
00:24:25,273 --> 00:24:28,539
You, even as a person, would have
to read pretty closely to notice

409
00:24:28,539 --> 00:24:31,943
them, and so when the judge has,
like, 40 things to think about, you

410
00:24:31,949 --> 00:24:33,788
could see how it might lose those.

411
00:24:34,529 --> 00:24:36,978
and reading is sort of the first
thing to go, it seems like.

412
00:24:37,431 --> 00:24:45,681
Now, on the other side, a cutoff ending, a
cutoff ending and wrong numbers got easier

413
00:24:45,682 --> 00:24:47,419
to catch with more metrics per call.

414
00:24:47,467 --> 00:24:51,250
So that's really crazy
as well and unexpected.

415
00:24:51,356 --> 00:24:54,877
I'm thinking maybe that the extra
metrics include things like completeness

416
00:24:54,881 --> 00:25:00,907
and accuracy, and those nudge the
judge to check whether the answer is

417
00:25:00,910 --> 00:25:02,796
whole and whether the facts hold up.

418
00:25:03,454 --> 00:25:07,214
So this might be something that
improves the reasoning of the

419
00:25:07,214 --> 00:25:12,408
judge by having it consider more
aspects of the particular, request.

420
00:25:13,286 --> 00:25:15,108
And the rest kind of barely moved.

421
00:25:15,162 --> 00:25:18,902
shuffled sentences, flattened
formatting, filler phrases, the

422
00:25:18,903 --> 00:25:22,553
deleted answer, these all kind of
came out about where they started.

423
00:25:23,368 --> 00:25:28,904
So I guess the takeaway from this is that
packing doesn't hurt every metric equally.

424
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:33,342
Like, if your rubric depends on
s- catching small details, tone,

425
00:25:33,352 --> 00:25:37,332
grammar, repetition, those are the
ones that you wanna watch the closest

426
00:25:37,350 --> 00:25:41,951
as your rubric grows and maybe run
those as their own calls, not in

427
00:25:41,953 --> 00:25:44,428
the overall, packed model call.

428
00:25:45,428 --> 00:25:48,824
Okay, so I just wanna tell you a
little bit about how I measured this.

429
00:25:49,658 --> 00:25:53,323
every prompt in the big test
was scored at all six sizes.

430
00:25:53,362 --> 00:26:00,583
Same prompt, same edit, one metric, then
five, then 10, all the way up to 40, so

431
00:26:00,583 --> 00:26:05,080
that each prompt got its own line, like
the ones I'm showing here in this slide.

432
00:26:06,543 --> 00:26:09,903
I'm not comparing one group of prompts
against another group of prompts.

433
00:26:09,916 --> 00:26:14,951
I'm comparing each prompt with itself,
essentially, and this matters a lot

434
00:26:14,963 --> 00:26:19,285
because some of the prompts are,
like, a lot harder than the others.

435
00:26:19,337 --> 00:26:23,586
some are easy, and if you compare
prompts, that will add a lot of noise.

436
00:26:24,003 --> 00:26:30,141
so when you need a huge sample
to see anything, like, that's

437
00:26:30,141 --> 00:26:31,521
gonna make it even harder for you.

438
00:26:31,727 --> 00:26:35,666
So when each prompt is sort of its
own control, the hard and easy cases

439
00:26:35,681 --> 00:26:40,113
end up canceling out, and the small
effect of packing will show up clearly.

440
00:26:41,424 --> 00:26:44,464
It's kind of like benchmarking,
a service on the same machine

441
00:26:44,465 --> 00:26:46,008
before and after a change, right?

442
00:26:46,266 --> 00:26:49,447
You wouldn't run the test on
one box and then another test

443
00:26:49,470 --> 00:26:50,941
on another and trust the result.

444
00:26:50,955 --> 00:26:52,623
Like, you wanna kinda keep it consistent.

445
00:26:53,340 --> 00:26:57,182
And the code on the right
is that idea in four lines.

446
00:26:58,733 --> 00:27:03,341
It lines up each prompt score at one
metric with its score at 40, drops

447
00:27:03,358 --> 00:27:06,187
any prompt that's missing either
one, and takes the difference.

